1. Resource Management:
    - In Kubernetes, Resource Management means:
    - “How much CPU and RAM each container/pod is allowed to use.”
    - Think of a Kubernetes cluster like a big apartment building.
        Pods = tenants
        CPU/RAM = electricity + water
        Kubernetes must stop one tenant from consuming everything.
    - That’s where:
        Requests
        Limits
        LimitRange
        ResourceQuota
    come in.

2. Requests & Limits
    - These are set on containers inside a Pod.
    - Requests = Minimum Guaranteed Resources
        This tells Kubernetes:
        “Please reserve this much CPU/RAM for me.”
        Example:
            resources:
                requests:
                    memory: "256Mi"
                    cpu: "200m"
        Meaning:
            Reserve:
                256MB RAM
                0.2 CPU core
        Kubernetes uses this while scheduling Pods.
        If a node doesn’t have enough free resources,
        the Pod will NOT be placed there.

    - Limits = Maximum Allowed Resources
        This tells Kubernetes:
        “Do not let me cross this limit.”
        Example:
        resources:
            limits:
                memory: "512Mi"
                cpu: "500m"
        Meaning:
            Max RAM = 512MB
            Max CPU = 0.5 core

    - Real-Life Analogy
    Imagine a hotel buffet.
        Request = reserved food for you
        Limit = maximum plates you can take
    So:
        Type	Meaning
        Request	Minimum guaranteed
        Limit	Maximum allowed

3. What Happens If Limit Crosses:
    - CPU Limit Crossed
    - Kubernetes throttles/slows the container.
        Like: “Bro, slow down.”
    - Memory Limit Crossed
        Container gets killed with:
            OOMKilled
        (OOM = Out Of Memory)
        Because RAM cannot be safely overused.
        - Full Example
            apiVersion: v1
            kind: Pod
            metadata:
                name: nginx
            spec:
                containers:
                - name: nginx
                    image: nginx
                resources:
                    requests:
                        memory: "128Mi"
                        cpu: "100m"
                    limits:
                        memory: "256Mi"
                        cpu: "500m"
    - CPU Units Explained
    1000m = 1 CPU core
    - Examples:
        Value	Meaning
        100m	0.1 CPU
        500m	0.5 CPU
        1000m	1 CPU
    - Memory Units Explained
        Value	Meaning
        Mi	Mebibyte
        Gi	Gibibyte
    - Examples:
        Value	Approx
        128Mi	128MB
        1Gi	    1024MB

4. LimitRange
    - A LimitRange sets default/min/max resource rules inside a Namespace.
    - Think: “Every Pod in this namespace must follow these rules.”
    
    - Why Needed
    Sometimes developers forget to add:
        resources:
    Without limits:
        One Pod may consume all RAM
        Cluster becomes unstable
    LimitRange prevents this.
    - Example
        apiVersion: v1
        kind: LimitRange
        metadata:
            name: mem-limit-range
        spec:
            limits:
            - default:
                memory: 512Mi
                cpu: 500m
            defaultRequest:
                memory: 256Mi
                cpu: 200m
            type: Container

    - What This Does
    If developer creates Pod WITHOUT resources:
    Kubernetes automatically applies:
        requests:
            memory: 256Mi
            cpu: 200m
        limits:
            memory: 512Mi
            cpu: 500m
    - LimitRange Can Also Restrict
    Example:
        max:
            memory: 1Gi
        min:
            memory: 128Mi
    Meaning:
        cannot exceed 1Gi
        cannot go below 128Mi

5. ResourceQuota
    - ResourceQuota controls TOTAL resource usage for a Namespace.
        - Think: “This team can only use this much overall.”
    - Real-Life Analogy
        Company office:
            Team A gets:
            8 CPUs
            16GB RAM
            20 Pods
        Even if they create many apps,
        they cannot cross that quota.
    - Example
        apiVersion: v1
        kind: ResourceQuota
        metadata:
            name: team-a-quota
        spec:
            hard:
                requests.cpu: "4"
                requests.memory: 4Gi
                limits.cpu: "8"
                limits.memory: 8Gi
                pods: "10"
    - Meaning
    Namespace total cannot exceed:
        Resource	    Limit
        Requested CPU	4 cores
        Requested RAM	4Gi
        CPU Limits	    8 cores
        Memory Limits	8Gi
        Pods	        10

    - If Quota Exceeded?
        Pod creation fails.
        Example error:
            exceeded quota
    - Big Picture
        Cluster
        ├── Namespace A
        │     ├── ResourceQuota
        │     ├── LimitRange
        │     └── Pods
        │
        └── Namespace B

6. Difference Between LimitRange vs ResourceQuota
    Feature	    LimitRange	                ResourceQuota
    Applies To	Individual Pod/Container	Entire Namespace
    Purpose	    Default/min/max values	    Total usage control
    Prevents	Bad Pod configs	            Team overconsumption

7. QoS (Quality of Service) Classes:
    - In Kubernetes, QoS (Quality of Service) classes define how Pods are prioritized when the node is under pressure (CPU/memory shortage).
    - There are 3 QoS classes:
        - Guaranteed (highest priority)
        - Burstable (middle)
        - BestEffort (lowest priority)
    - Kubernetes uses these during evictions (when node runs out of memory/CPU).

    - 1. Guaranteed QoS (Top Priority)
        - Rule: Every container in the Pod must have:
            CPU request = CPU limit
            Memory request = Memory limit
            AND all containers must define both
        - Example:
            apiVersion: v1
            kind: Pod
            metadata:
                name: guaranteed-pod
            spec:
                containers:
                - name: app
                    image: nginx
                    resources:
                        requests:
                            cpu: "500m"
                            memory: "256Mi"
                        limits:
                            cpu: "500m"
                            memory: "256Mi"
    
    - 2. Burstable QoS (Middle Priority)
        - Rule:
            Requests and limits are defined
            BUT they are NOT equal OR multiple containers vary
        - Example:
            apiVersion: v1
            kind: Pod
            metadata:
                name: burstable-pod
            spec:
                containers:
                - name: app
                    image: nginx
                    resources:
                        requests:
                            cpu: "200m"
                            memory: "128Mi"
                        limits:
                            cpu: "1"
                            memory: "512Mi"
    
    - 3. BestEffort QoS (Lowest Priority)
        - Rule:
            NO requests
            NO limits
        - Example:
            apiVersion: v1
            kind: Pod
            metadata:
                name: besteffort-pod
            spec:
                containers:
                - name: app
                    image: nginx